README.OSK -- how this tree differs from 4.3BSD-Net/2's usr/src/games/atcORIG/ is the tree exactly as Net/2 carried it: atc, Ed James's air trafficcontroller game (UC Berkeley, 1987), in the University of California's 1990release.  obj (a symlink into the build machine) and tags are not carried.The game itself is unchanged.  It is built on the source's own SYSV path,which already uses alarm(), strchr(), memcpy() and rand(), with -DOSKguards where OS-9 has no SYSV equivalent:  include.h    no <unistd.h> or <sys/utsname.h>; sgttyb holds only the erase               and kill characters input.c edits with; SIGALRM is 5, the               signal unix.l's alarm() sends through F$Alarm -- the system               <signal.h> names no SIGALRM.  main.c       curses cbreak() and noecho() in place of the termio ioctls;               erase is backspace and kill is ^X; SIGTERM only if defined.  input.c      the `!' shell escape is left out; that key only redraws.  graphics.c   getAChar() retries a read a signal interrupted.  On OS-9               the signal aborts the pending read with its own code (5               for the alarm, 3 for ^C) as the error, and stdio's error               flag stays set until clearerr() -- left alone, every later               read failed at once and the game beeped without end.  It               also calls unix.l's check_signal(): that library's signal()               only records a signal, and the handler -- update(), the               game clock, or quit() -- runs when the program asks.  log.c        no umask(); open() on OS-9 does not create, so a missing               score file is made with creat(); no lockf(); the player's               name comes from USER, not getpwuid() -- blarslib.l's reads               the password file, which need not list the running user,               and the game then logged no score; the host column               reads "OS-9"; the 100-entry score list (53K) is allocated               with malloc().  On the stack it lies past what a 16-bit               displacement reaches (the assembler stops with `value out               of range'), and as static data it takes the module past               l68's 64K (`non-remote data allocation ... exceeds 64k').  pathnames.h  the airport files and atc_score are in /dd/GAMES/ATC/.  extern.c     supplies yywrap(), which this disk's flex skeleton leaves to               the program on OS-9 (there is no lex library).  include.h    also renames the game's update() to atc_update(): curses.l               has an update() of its own, and l68 stops on the clash.grammar.c, y.tab.h and lex.c are generated, and they were generated ONOS-9 by this disk's own tools:    yacc -d grammar.y             (writes y.tab.c, kept as grammar.c)    flex -S/dd/LIB/flex.skel lex.l (writes lex.yy.c, kept as lex.c)The recipe links unix.l for alarm() and blarslib.l for atan2().games/ATC_scores is the distribution's sample score file; the programnever reads that name, so it is kept here only.